#version 450

// Injects the CURRENT sun's first bounce into the probe grid, by marching the same voxel geometry
// the visibility bake traced.
//
// ── Why this is a pass and not a bake ────────────────────────────────────────
// Baked sky visibility told the floor it sees 0.15 of the sky where the renderer had been giving it
// 0.85 — correct, and on its own it made the courtyard black. What actually lights a courtyard is
// sunlight off its own lit walls, and the sun arrives at irradiance 17 against an ambient of well
// under one. That term cannot be baked: move the sun and every lit wall changes. So the geometry is
// precomputed and the lighting is solved here, which is the split the roadmap asked for.
//
// ── No per-voxel material, and none needed ───────────────────────────────────
// A ray that hits takes the voxel FACE it entered through as the surface normal — exact for an
// axis-aligned voxel, and the DDA already knows which axis it last stepped. From that hit a second
// march runs toward the sun: unoccluded means the surface is lit, and it re-radiates. Storing an
// albedo and a normal per voxel would be 24 MB to say something this derives for free.
//
// ── Multi-bounce, because one is not enough for a courtyard ─────────────────
// A point three metres up in Sponza's courtyard sees about three of ninety-six ray directions as
// sky — measured, and matching the analytic solid angle of a 21-degree cone between sixteen-metre
// walls. Light down there has bounced several times by the time it arrives, so a single bounce off
// walls that are themselves nearly enclosed cannot fill it.
//
// So a hit surface re-radiates the sun it catches PLUS whatever the previous sweep already left
// there. Reading the texture this pass writes is deliberate: probes refresh on a rotation, so a
// read almost always sees a settled neighbour, and each full sweep advances the solution by one
// bounce. Albedo multiplies in every time, so the series converges rather than runs away.
//
// ── What the probe stores, and why the units matter ─────────────────────────
// Average incident RADIANCE over the sphere, not irradiance. The distinction decides whether the
// feedback loop converges: with each ray contributing albedo/PI and the sum then scaled by 4*PI/N,
// the gain per bounce came to albedo*4 = 1.4, and a gain above one is a runaway. Measured, it lit
// the scene to four times its original brightness.
//
// Kept as radiance the accounting is simply: a surface's incident irradiance is PI times the
// average radiance reaching it, its outgoing radiance is albedo times that over PI, and the probe
// averages what arrives. The albedo is then the whole per-bounce gain, so the series converges for
// any physical material. The consumer multiplies by PI to get irradiance back.
//
// ── L0 only ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RGB radiance, no direction. Bounce is the low-frequency half of the image and a directional
// bounce is four times the storage for a term that is about to be added to a cosine lobe anyway.
// The visibility volume beside it IS directional, so the SHAPE of the ambient still comes from
// geometry; this supplies its colour and level.

#include "probe_volume.glsl"
#include "sky_visibility.glsl"

#define BLIX_OCT_INTERIOR 6
#define BLIX_OCT_TILE     8
// <b>256, and the reason is the DEPTH map rather than the irradiance.</b> Irradiance averages every
// ray under a cosine lobe and was well fed at 64: measured, 24.0 effective samples per texel. Depth
// uses a pow(dot, 50) lobe to keep a wall's edge an edge, and that lobe is so tight that 64 rays over
// 36 texels gave it <b>1.82 effective samples</b> — each depth texel was essentially ONE ray.
//
// That breaks the visibility test in the specific way this file's own comment forbids: the mean is
// one ray's hit distance, so whether a probe behind a wall gets rejected depends on whether its
// single ray in that direction struck the wall or slipped past it; and the variance, which is the
// entire reason probe_volume.glsl chose Chebyshev over a hard depth test, is the variance of one
// sample — zero, clamped to 1e-5. The soft test the design asked for was a hard one all along.
//
// 256 rays take it to 4.97. The cheaper alternative — widening the lobe to pow 8, which gives 6.72
// at 64 rays — was rejected because it buys the samples by blurring exactly the edge the exponent
// exists to preserve, and the edge is what the test reads.
#define BLIX_OCT_RAYS     256

// How far one solve moves a stored tile toward its new answer. 1.0 is the hard replace this had.
#define SOLVE_CONVERGE 0.4


// One workgroup per probe; one thread per ray, then one thread per tile texel. The rays are
// marched once into shared memory and every texel reads all of them, which is what makes a
// 36-texel map cost one set of marches rather than thirty-six.
layout(local_size_x = BLIX_OCT_RAYS) in;

shared vec3 sRadiance[BLIX_OCT_RAYS];
shared vec3 sDirection[BLIX_OCT_RAYS];
shared vec3 sTexel[BLIX_OCT_INTERIOR * BLIX_OCT_INTERIOR];
shared float sClosure[BLIX_OCT_RAYS];
shared float sMeanClosure;
shared float sDistance[BLIX_OCT_RAYS];
// Per-ray reachability, reduced once per probe below.
shared float sReach[BLIX_OCT_RAYS];
shared float sSunlit[BLIX_OCT_RAYS];
shared vec2 sDepthTexel[BLIX_OCT_INTERIOR * BLIX_OCT_INTERIOR];

layout(set = 0, binding = 0) uniform Inject {
    vec4 uBoundsMin;      // xyz world min of the probe/occupancy volume, w unused
    vec4 uBoundsSpan;     // xyz world span, w = bounce albedo
    vec4 uProbeDims;      // xyz probe grid dims, w = rays per probe
    vec4 uOccupancyDims;  // xyz occupancy dims, w unused
    vec4 uSunDirection;   // xyz direction the sun TRAVELS, w unused
    vec4 uSunIrradiance;  // rgb measured irradiance, w = 1 when the sky-visibility volume is loaded
    // x = frame counter, y = how many frames a full sweep takes. Bounce is the low-frequency half
    // of the image and the sun moves slowly, so solving every probe every frame spends 19 ms to
    // re-derive a field that barely changed — measured, it doubled the frame. Each probe is
    // refreshed on its own turn instead.
    // x = frame counter, y = sweep length, z = usage decay per frame (0 disables sleeping).
    vec4 uSchedule;   // w: 1 when the atlases alternate and a skipped probe must carry its tile
    // xyz albedo grid dims (0 = none shipped), w = translucency.
    vec4 uAlbedoDims;
} u;

// <b>An octahedral atlas, not four coefficients.</b> L1 is one smooth lobe over the sphere, so a
// source subtending twenty degrees and one subtending ninety give the same SHAPE at different
// magnitudes — measured on the cypress, teal curtains metres away washed its lower half because L1
// could say they were there and not how wide they were. A map discretises instead of projecting, so
// sharpness becomes resolution rather than basis order.
//
// One tile per probe, 8x8 with a one-texel border ring around a 6x6 interior. The border is not
// decoration: tiles are sampled bilinearly, and across an octahedral fold the neighbour is the texel
// diagonally opposite rather than the one beside it. Without it every probe carries a seam that
// moves with the camera.
//
// Tiles are laid out x across, (y + z * dimY) down — so a probe's neighbours in x are adjacent in
// memory, which is the axis a trilinear fetch walks first.
layout(set = 0, binding = 1, rgba16f) uniform image2D uAtlas;
layout(set = 0, binding = 4) uniform sampler2D uAtlasPrev;
// Mean and mean-square distance per direction — the half of a probe that says whether it can SEE a
// shading point, as opposed to how bright it is. Same tile layout, so one set of coordinate maths.
layout(set = 0, binding = 11, rgba16f) uniform image2D uDepthAtlas;
layout(set = 0, binding = 12) uniform sampler2D uDepthAtlasPrev;
layout(set = 0, binding = 2) uniform sampler3D uOccupancy;
// <b>The sky, as a SOURCE in the transport chain rather than only a term at the end.</b> These
// were bound and unused: uSkyVisibility was declared here and never read once. The bounce solve
// took its energy from `sun * sunReach + previousBounce`, so the only thing that could start a
// chain was a SUNLIT surface. That is enough to bring a courtyard alive and not nearly enough for
// an arcade: a corridor that sees little sun is lit almost entirely by sky arriving through an
// opening and then bouncing twice more off warm stone, and none of that existed. The interiors
// were not failing to sample their light, they had never been given any.
layout(set = 0, binding = 3) uniform sampler3D uSkyVisibility;
layout(set = 0, binding = 6) uniform sampler3D uSkyVisibility1;
layout(set = 0, binding = 8) uniform sampler3D uSkyVisibility2;
// The distant sky's cosine-convolved irradiance, the same cube the lit pass reads.
layout(set = 0, binding = 9) uniform samplerCube uIrradiance;
layout(set = 0, binding = 5) uniform sampler3D uAlbedo;
// <b>Which probes shading actually read, written by the fragment stage.</b> No bake-time test can
// tell a probe inside the model's shell from one in the arcade — measured, they are geometrically
// identical and the occupancy grid is not even watertight. What separates them is that nothing
// renders against one of them, and that is a fact only the fragment shader holds.
layout(set = 0, binding = 10, rgba16f) uniform image3D uProbeUsage;

#define PI 3.14159265359
// What the previous sweep left at a point, for a surface facing n. One bilinear fetch inside that
// probe's tile — the border ring is what makes this fetch safe at the octahedron's fold.
vec3 blixSampleAtlas(vec3 worldPos, vec3 n) {
    ivec3 dims = ivec3(u.uProbeDims.xyz);
    vec3 grid = clamp((worldPos - u.uBoundsMin.xyz) / u.uBoundsSpan.xyz, vec3(0.0), vec3(1.0))
              * vec3(dims) - 0.5;
    ivec3 p = clamp(ivec3(floor(grid + 0.5)), ivec3(0), dims - 1);

    vec2 oct = blix_octEncode(normalize(n)) * 0.5 + 0.5;          // [0,1] across the interior
    vec2 tile = vec2(p.x, p.y + p.z * dims.y) * float(BLIX_OCT_TILE);
    vec2 texel = tile + 1.0 + oct * float(BLIX_OCT_INTERIOR);      // +1 skips the border ring
    vec2 atlasSize = vec2(dims.x, dims.y * dims.z) * float(BLIX_OCT_TILE);
    return texture(uAtlasPrev, texel / atlasSize).rgb;
}

// The grid stores DENSITY, not occupancy. The baker writes 1.0 for an opaque triangle and
// CutoutOpacity for an alpha-cutout one, precisely so that a canopy attenuates instead of walling,
// and its own Transmittance() reads it that way. Reading it back here through a `> 0.5` test threw
// that grade away and made every leaf INVISIBLE to this march: rays passed clean through the tree,
// escaped, and reported sky — which lit the inside of the canopy as though it were open air. Same
// texture, same decision, now taken the same way on both sides of it.
float densityAt(ivec3 c) {
    float d = texelFetch(uOccupancy, clamp(c, ivec3(0), ivec3(u.uOccupancyDims.xyz) - 1), 0).r;
    // uOccupancyDims.w = 0 restores the old boolean read, so the two can be compared by turning a
    // control rather than by rebuilding. An opaque cell reads 1.0 either way; only foliage differs.
    return u.uOccupancyDims.w > 0.5 ? d : (d > 0.5 ? 1.0 : 0.0);
}

// What the surface in this cell is coloured, linear. Filtered rather than fetched: the grid is
// half the occupancy resolution, so a nearest read would step in visible blocks across a wall that
// the density grid resolves smoothly. Squared on the way in — the byte holds sqrt(linear), which is
// what keeps a curtain's absorbing channels from quantising to a handful of codes.
vec3 albedoAt(vec3 worldPos) {
    if (u.uAlbedoDims.x < 1.0) return vec3(1.0);   // older .blixsky: the scalar alone, as before
    vec3 uvw = (worldPos - u.uBoundsMin.xyz) / u.uBoundsSpan.xyz;
    vec3 c = texture(uAlbedo, clamp(uvw, vec3(0.0), vec3(1.0))).rgb;
    return c * c;
}

// The DDA itself is shared, because the two walks below differ only in what they do per cell, and
// a second hand-inlined copy of this is how the two sides drifted apart in the first place.
struct Dda {
    ivec3 c;
    ivec3 step;
    vec3 tMax;
    vec3 inv;
    int axis;
};

Dda ddaInit(vec3 origin, vec3 dir, vec3 dims, vec3 cell) {
    Dda d;
    vec3 p = (origin - u.uBoundsMin.xyz) / cell;
    d.c = clamp(ivec3(floor(p)), ivec3(0), ivec3(dims) - 1);
    d.step = ivec3(sign(dir));
    d.inv = 1.0 / max(abs(dir), vec3(1e-9));
    d.tMax = vec3(
        (d.step.x > 0 ? (float(d.c.x) + 1.0 - p.x) : (p.x - float(d.c.x))) * d.inv.x,
        (d.step.y > 0 ? (float(d.c.y) + 1.0 - p.y) : (p.y - float(d.c.y))) * d.inv.y,
        (d.step.z > 0 ? (float(d.c.z) + 1.0 - p.z) : (p.z - float(d.c.z))) * d.inv.z);
    d.axis = 0;
    return d;
}

// Returns false once the walk has left the grid, which is the one case that means "sky".
bool ddaStep(inout Dda d, vec3 dims) {
    if (d.tMax.x < d.tMax.y && d.tMax.x < d.tMax.z) { d.c.x += d.step.x; d.tMax.x += d.inv.x; d.axis = 0; }
    else if (d.tMax.y < d.tMax.z)                   { d.c.y += d.step.y; d.tMax.y += d.inv.y; d.axis = 1; }
    else                                            { d.c.z += d.step.z; d.tMax.z += d.inv.z; d.axis = 2; }
    return !(any(lessThan(d.c, ivec3(0))) || any(greaterThanEqual(d.c, ivec3(dims))));
}

// How much sunlight survives the walk toward the sun. The boolean version asked "did anything stop
// the ray", which for a leaf card is the wrong question — letting light through is half of what a
// canopy does.
// Spectral, not scalar, and that is the whole point of it. A leaf does not dim sunlight evenly —
// it absorbs red and passes green — so a float here can only ever deliver grey light to the ground
// under a tree. Light reaching the far side of a cell is the part that went through the gaps
// (untinted, 1 - density) plus the part that went through the material itself, wearing its colour.
vec3 transmittance(vec3 origin, vec3 dir, float maxCells) {
    vec3 dims = u.uOccupancyDims.xyz;
    vec3 cell = u.uBoundsSpan.xyz / dims;
    Dda d = ddaInit(origin, dir, dims, cell);
    vec3 t = vec3(1.0);
    for (int i = 0; i < int(maxCells); ++i) {
        if (!ddaStep(d, dims)) break;   // left the grid: the rest of the way is open sky
        float density = densityAt(d.c);
        if (density <= 0.01) continue;
        // An OPAQUE cell transmits nothing, whatever colour it is. Without this gate a wall at
        // density 1 would pass albedo * translucency and the sun would come through stone.
        float trans = density >= 0.99 ? 0.0 : u.uAlbedoDims.w;
        vec3 through = vec3(1.0 - density);
        if (trans > 0.0) {
            vec3 hit = u.uBoundsMin.xyz + (vec3(d.c) + 0.5) * cell;
            through += density * albedoAt(hit) * trans;
        }
        t *= through;
        if (dot(t, vec3(1.0)) < 0.03) return vec3(0.0);
    }
    return t;
}

// Walk one direction and accumulate what it passes, rather than stopping at the first thing it
// hits. An opaque wall still ends the walk on its first cell — t drops to zero there — so this
// costs nothing wherever the old version was already right. A canopy now contributes each leaf the
// ray crosses, weighted by how much light is still travelling when it arrives.
// Returns the contribution as radiance; the caller projects it onto SH using the ray's direction,
// which is the direction that light arrived FROM and the whole reason this became directional.
// <b>reach: 1 if this ray left the volume without meeting anything, sunlit: 1 if any cell it met
// could see the sun.</b> Together, over a probe's whole ray set, they say whether light has any
// route to this probe at all — which is a different question from how much light it holds, and the
// one nothing previously asked.
vec3 gather(vec3 origin, vec3 dir, float maxCells, vec3 toSun, float cellSize,
            out float hitDistance, out float reach, out float sunlit, out float closure) {
    // <b>How much of this ray's unit of transport was handed to a surface.</b> A self-consistency
    // number and the only kind this solve can check without an external reference: the march starts
    // with a transmittance of one, spends `t * density` at every cell that re-radiates, and this is
    // the total it spent. A ray ending in opaque geometry must close to 1; a ray that escapes to sky
    // closes to 0, correctly, because the sky's direct contribution is the lit pass's IBL term and
    // not the probe field's job. So the EXPECTED mean over a probe's rays is 1 - its sky visibility,
    // and the bake computed that independently — two separate calculations of the same geometry,
    // which is what makes disagreement between them evidence rather than opinion.
    closure = 0.0;
    // <b>How far the ray got, which is the other half of a probe.</b> Radiance alone cannot say
    // whether a probe is behind a wall from some shading point's perspective; distance can, and the
    // march already knows it. Escaping rays report the far bound rather than zero — an open
    // direction is infinitely far, not immediately blocked, and zero would read as a wall.
    hitDistance = maxCells * length(u.uBoundsSpan.xyz / u.uOccupancyDims.xyz);
    vec3 dims = u.uOccupancyDims.xyz;
    vec3 cell = u.uBoundsSpan.xyz / dims;
    Dda d = ddaInit(origin, dir, dims, cell);
    vec3 sum = vec3(0.0);
    vec3 t = vec3(1.0);
    bool recordedHit = false;
    reach = 1.0;    // cleared only by SOLID geometry -- see below
    sunlit = 0.0;

    for (int i = 0; i < int(maxCells); ++i) {
        if (!ddaStep(d, dims)) break;   // escaped: that is sky, already baked into the visibility SH
        float density = densityAt(d.c);
        if (density <= 0.01) continue;

        // The FIRST thing this ray meets is what stands between a probe and anything beyond it, so
        // the distance to it is what the visibility test needs — not the distance to whatever
        // happened to contribute most of the light.
        if (!recordedHit) {
            hitDistance = distance(origin, u.uBoundsMin.xyz + (vec3(d.c) + 0.5) * cell);
            recordedHit = true;
        }
        // <b>Only SOLID geometry seals a space; a canopy never does.</b> This cleared on any
        // density at all, so a probe inside the lower half of the cypress -- surrounded by cells at
        // 0.34 -- was counted as enclosed and rejected, while the sunlit top half passed. Reported
        // from the chair as exactly that split, and it is wrong: leaves are a filter, not a wall.
        // Dappled light reaches under a tree, and rejecting those probes deletes it.
        //
        // The transmittance through ten foliage cells is 0.66^10 = 1.5%, which is under the 2%
        // threshold below -- so foliage could seal a volume by arithmetic that stone would need a
        // closed room to achieve.
        if (density >= 0.99) reach = 0.0;

        // A cell absorbs this fraction of the light still travelling, and re-radiates it. Spectral
        // for the same reason as the walk toward the sun: what arrives here has already passed
        // through whatever stands between, and that may have been a leaf.
        vec3 weight = t * density;
        // Counted before the 0.06 threshold below, on purpose: a cell skipped for being too faint
        // to matter is still transport that happened, and hiding it here would make the instrument
        // agree with the shortcut instead of measuring it.
        closure += dot(weight, vec3(1.0)) / 3.0;
        float trans = density >= 0.99 ? 0.0 : u.uAlbedoDims.w;

        // Below this a cell moves the answer by less than the 32-frame rotation's own noise, and
        // each one that clears the bar costs a second march. This bound is why foliage stays cheap.
        if (dot(weight, vec3(1.0)) > 0.06) {
            vec3 hitPos = u.uBoundsMin.xyz + (vec3(d.c) + 0.5) * cell;
            // The face we came through: opposite the axis we stepped along.
            vec3 n = vec3(0.0);
            n[d.axis] = float(-d.step[d.axis]);

            // A surface facing away from the sun catches none of it, and asking the grid about a
            // ray whose answer is already zero was the old loop's one plain waste.
            float ndotl = max(dot(n, toSun), 0.0);
            vec3 sunReach = ndotl > 0.0
                ? transmittance(hitPos + n * cellSize * 1.5, toSun, maxCells) * ndotl
                : vec3(0.0);
            if (dot(sunReach, vec3(1.0)) > 1e-4) sunlit = 1.0;

            // Sampled just off the face, so the lookup is not taken inside the geometry it stands on.
            vec3 prevUv = (hitPos + n * cellSize * 1.5 - u.uBoundsMin.xyz) / u.uBoundsSpan.xyz;
            // <b>Evaluated along the hit surface's OWN normal.</b> The directionless version could
            // only ask "how much light is around here"; a surface re-radiates what reaches the side
            // it faces, and now it can be asked that.
            // Evaluated along the hit surface's OWN normal: a surface re-radiates what reaches the
            // side it faces, and the atlas can finally be asked that at full directional resolution.
            // <b>Three sources now: sun, sky, and the previous bounce.</b> The sky term is the
            // distant irradiance cube along this surface's own normal, scaled by how much sky the
            // baked volume says it can actually see from here — the same pair the lit pass uses,
            // asked at the hit instead of at the camera. A wall under an arcade opening therefore
            // re-radiates the sky it receives, and the corridor behind it gets a second and third
            // bounce of that light rather than nothing.
            //
            // Queried at the already-offset position with no further push: hitPos is one and a half
            // occupancy cells off the face, which is what the wall bias is for.
            vec3 probePos = hitPos + n * cellSize * 1.5;
            vec3 skyTerm = u.uSunIrradiance.w > 0.5
                ? texture(uIrradiance, n).rgb
                    * blix_skyVisibility(uSkyVisibility, uSkyVisibility1, uSkyVisibility2,
                                         u.uBoundsMin.xyz, 1.0 / u.uBoundsSpan.xyz, 0.0,
                                         probePos, n)
                : vec3(0.0);
            // <b>The full eight-probe blend, kept, and the measurement is why.</b> This looks like
            // the expensive thing in the loop — a trilinear blend with a Chebyshev visibility test
            // on each of eight probes, roughly sixteen fetches, taken at every cell every ray
            // touches — and it was tried three ways. Against a nearest-probe fetch it is worth
            // 5.06 ms of 36.1, and against no feedback at all 5.35: fifteen percent of the pass,
            // not the bulk of it. In the shipped configuration, where sleeping keeps most probes
            // out of the solve, that is about 0.6 ms.
            //
            // And the cheap version LEAKS. Dropping to the nearest probe skips the Chebyshev test,
            // which is precisely what stops a probe behind a wall contributing — the field's median
            // rose from 0.0521 to 0.0530 when it was removed. Trading 0.6 ms for light coming
            // through walls is a worse bargain than the milliseconds suggest.
            vec3 incident = u.uSunIrradiance.rgb * sunReach + skyTerm
                          + blix_probeIrradiance(uAtlasPrev, uDepthAtlasPrev, ivec3(u.uProbeDims.xyz),
                                                 u.uBoundsMin.xyz, u.uBoundsSpan.xyz, probePos, n);

            // Lambertian outgoing radiance, now in the surface's OWN colour. uBoundsSpan.w stops
            // being the scene's albedo and becomes a plain strength multiplier over it.
            sum += weight * u.uBoundsSpan.w * albedoAt(hitPos) * incident / PI;
        }

        // Tinted for the same reason the sun walk is: what continues past a red curtain is red.
        {
            vec3 through = vec3(1.0 - density);
            if (trans > 0.0) {
                vec3 here = u.uBoundsMin.xyz + (vec3(d.c) + 0.5) * cell;
                through += density * albedoAt(here) * trans;
            }
            t *= through;
        }
        if (dot(t, vec3(1.0)) < 0.03) break;
    }
    return sum;
}

vec3 fibonacci(int i, int count) {
    float y = 1.0 - 2.0 * (float(i) + 0.5) / float(count);
    float r = sqrt(max(0.0, 1.0 - y * y));
    float theta = 2.39996323 * float(i);
    return vec3(cos(theta) * r, y, sin(theta) * r);
}

// Where probe (x,y,z)'s tile starts in the atlas.
ivec2 blixTileOrigin(ivec3 probe, ivec3 dims) {
    return ivec2(probe.x, probe.y + probe.z * dims.y) * BLIX_OCT_TILE;
}

void main() {
    ivec3 dims = ivec3(u.uProbeDims.xyz);
    int probeIndex = int(gl_WorkGroupID.x);
    ivec3 probe = ivec3(probeIndex % dims.x,
                        (probeIndex / dims.x) % dims.y,
                        probeIndex / (dims.x * dims.y));
    if (probe.z >= dims.z) return;

    int lane = int(gl_LocalInvocationIndex);
    ivec2 tile = blixTileOrigin(probe, dims);
    // <b>A lane only owns a texel while there are as many lanes as texels.</b> This mapping is exact
    // at 64 lanes over an 8x8 tile and silently catastrophic above it: lane 64 lands on row 8, which
    // is the NEXT probe's tile, so raising the ray count made every dispatch stomp its neighbours.
    // On screen that is not a subtle bias, it is a disco - and it invalidated the measurement taken
    // to judge the ray count, which duly reported the corruption as a worse light leak.
    //
    // The ray count and the texel count are now independent: rays are lanes, texels are the first
    // TILE*TILE lanes, and everything that writes the atlas is gated on owning one.
    const int tileTexels = BLIX_OCT_TILE * BLIX_OCT_TILE;
    bool ownsTexel = lane < tileTexels;
    ivec2 texel = ivec2(lane % BLIX_OCT_TILE, lane / BLIX_OCT_TILE);

    // Is this probe's turn? A scatter over the three axes rather than whole slices, so the ones
    // refreshed together are spread through the volume and a sun change arrives evenly rather than
    // sweeping across the scene as a visible plane.
    // <b>A probe nobody sampled is one nobody wanted lighting from.</b> Shading marks what it
    // reads; this decays that mark and stops solving once it reaches zero. The tile is still
    // carried forward, so a sleeping probe keeps its answer and wakes on its next turn rather than
    // flashing black — the sleep condition is cheap and the WAKE condition is the one that has to
    // be right.
    float usage = imageLoad(uProbeUsage, probe).r;
    float decay = u.uSchedule.z;
    bool asleep = decay > 0.0 && usage <= 0.02;

    int period = int(u.uSchedule.y);
    int slot = (probe.x * 5 + probe.y * 11 + probe.z * 17) % period;

    // <b>There was a wake stride here and it gated nothing.</b> It tested `asleep && !eligible`
    // and returned — but the very next test returns for `asleep` alone, so an eligible sleeping
    // probe took the second exit and behaved identically. Waking never passed through it: a probe
    // wakes when probe_usage marks it, which clears `asleep` before either test is reached. Deleted
    // rather than repaired, because the pop it was aimed at turned out to be the hard replace below.
    if (asleep || slot != int(u.uSchedule.x) % period) {
        // <b>Nothing to carry when there is only one atlas.</b> The copy below exists solely because
        // the pair alternates: a probe that simply returned was written into ONE of the two buffers
        // and stayed empty in the other, which read as the light swinging about between frames. With
        // a single atlas its last answer is already where every reader will look, and this becomes
        // the cheapest branch in the shader instead of the most expensive one in the pass.
        if (u.uSchedule.w > 0.5 && ownsTexel) {
            imageStore(uAtlas, tile + texel, texelFetch(uAtlasPrev, tile + texel, 0));
            imageStore(uDepthAtlas, tile + texel, texelFetch(uDepthAtlasPrev, tile + texel, 0));
        }
        if (lane == 0) imageStore(uProbeUsage, probe, vec4(max(usage - decay, 0.0)));
        return;
    }

    vec3 origin = u.uBoundsMin.xyz + (vec3(probe) + 0.5) * u.uBoundsSpan.xyz / vec3(dims);
    vec3 toSun = -normalize(u.uSunDirection.xyz);
    float maxCells = max(u.uOccupancyDims.x, max(u.uOccupancyDims.y, u.uOccupancyDims.z));
    float cellSize = length(u.uBoundsSpan.xyz / u.uOccupancyDims.xyz);

    // One ray per thread, marched once and shared with every texel that wants it.
    // <b>The count the dial actually asks for.</b> uProbeDims.w has carried "rays per probe" since
    // this shader was written and was never read: every probe marched all 256 lanes whatever the
    // overlay said, and the census printed "1,296 probes x 64 rays" — a number computed from a
    // slider with no effect. 256 is the ceiling, one ray per lane, so this can only reduce; lanes
    // past the count contribute nothing to the reconstruction but still own their texel and still
    // reach every barrier.
    int rays = clamp(int(u.uProbeDims.w + 0.5), 8, BLIX_OCT_RAYS);
    vec3 d = fibonacci(lane, rays);
    float rayDistance, rayReach, raySunlit, rayClosure;
    sDirection[lane] = d;
    rayDistance = 0.0; rayReach = 0.0; raySunlit = 0.0; rayClosure = 0.0;
    sRadiance[lane] = lane < rays
        ? gather(origin, d, maxCells, toSun, cellSize, rayDistance, rayReach, raySunlit, rayClosure)
        : vec3(0.0);
    sDistance[lane] = rayDistance;
    sReach[lane] = rayReach;
    sSunlit[lane] = raySunlit;
    sClosure[lane] = rayClosure;
    barrier();

    if (lane == 0) {
        float c = 0.0;
        for (int i = 0; i < rays; ++i) c += sClosure[i];
        sMeanClosure = c / float(rays);
    }
    barrier();

    // <b>Is there any route by which light reaches this probe at all?</b> Measured over the scene,
    // 38% of every surface's eight-probe blend weight targets probes it has no line of sight to,
    // and the Chebyshev test recovers only 39% of that — it cannot reject a probe just behind a
    // wall, because dist - mean is small there and var/(var + d^2) stays near 1. At 1.57 m spacing
    // against architectural walls that is the common case.
    //
    // This is the cheap half of the answer. A probe buried in geometry, or one that sees under 2%
    // of the sphere with no sun down any of its 64 rays, is in space light never enters — the
    // "army of boxes inside the shell of the model" a viewer spotted from the chair. It is not a
    // dim light source, it is not a light source. 7% of this grid (216 sealed + 132 buried of
    // 4,992) is in that state, and nothing downstream knew.
    //
    // Deliberately NOT a brightness test: a room lit only by a doorway is dim and entirely legitimate,
    // and thresholding on radiance would delete it. The test is geometric and asks about ROUTES.
    float escape = 0.0, litRays = 0.0;
    for (int i = 0; i < rays; ++i) { escape += sReach[i]; litRays += sSunlit[i]; }
    escape /= float(rays);
    // Its own cell: a probe inside a wall has no business lighting anything, whatever its rays say.
    vec3 selfUvw = (origin - u.uBoundsMin.xyz) / u.uBoundsSpan.xyz;
    bool buried = texture(uOccupancy, clamp(selfUvw, vec3(0.0), vec3(1.0))).r > 0.5;
    float reachable = (!buried && (escape >= 0.02 || litRays > 0.0)) ? 1.0 : 0.0;
    // <b>What share of the surfaces this probe can see is in sun.</b> The census says the sun
    // contributes LESS bounce to the field than the sky does, while being 7.6x brighter — which
    // would mean almost nothing a probe sees is lit, however bright the sun is. `sunlit` is already
    // computed per ray for the reachability test; summing it costs nothing and turns a suspicion
    // about the sun-ward voxel march into a number.
    float sunlitFraction = litRays / float(rays);

    // Interior texels: the cosine-weighted average of every ray, which IS the irradiance arriving
    // at a surface facing that texel's direction. Stored as irradiance so a reader multiplies by
    // albedo and nothing else — the PI that used to sit at the read site lives here now.
    const int interiorCount = BLIX_OCT_INTERIOR * BLIX_OCT_INTERIOR;
    if (lane < interiorCount) {
        ivec2 c = ivec2(lane % BLIX_OCT_INTERIOR, lane / BLIX_OCT_INTERIOR);
        vec2 uv = ((vec2(c) + 0.5) / float(BLIX_OCT_INTERIOR)) * 2.0 - 1.0;
        vec3 texelDir = blix_octDecode(uv);

        vec3 sum = vec3(0.0);
        float weight = 0.0;
        for (int i = 0; i < rays; ++i) {
            float w = max(dot(texelDir, sDirection[i]), 0.0);
            if (w <= 0.0) continue;
            sum += sRadiance[i] * w;
            weight += w;
        }
        sTexel[lane] = weight > 0.0 ? sum * (PI / weight) : vec3(0.0);

        // Depth wants a SHARPER lobe than irradiance. Irradiance is a cosine average by definition;
        // a distance field blurred over a hemisphere reports a wall as far away because most of the
        // hemisphere is open, and then fails to reject anything. The exponent is what keeps an edge
        // an edge.
        float dSum = 0.0, d2Sum = 0.0, dWeight = 0.0;
        for (int i = 0; i < rays; ++i) {
            float w = pow(max(dot(texelDir, sDirection[i]), 0.0), 50.0);
            if (w <= 0.0) continue;
            dSum += sDistance[i] * w;
            d2Sum += sDistance[i] * sDistance[i] * w;
            dWeight += w;
        }
        // <b>Variance, not the second moment — and the difference is the banding.</b> This stored
        // (mean, mean-square) and left the reader to form variance as mean2 - mean*mean. That is a
        // difference of two large nearly-equal numbers, and the reader takes both through a BILINEAR
        // fetch first, independently. The variance of two separately smoothed moments is not the
        // variance of anything; it is catastrophic cancellation, unstable enough that the consumer
        // has to clamp it off negative. Traced on one probe, it moved 0.35 to 2.06 across 6 cm of
        // surface, the cubed Chebyshev turned that into an 8x weight swing, and the probe flickered
        // in and out of the blend. Those switching contours are the bands on the stone.
        //
        // Differencing HERE, where both moments are exact and unfiltered, and storing the result
        // makes the interpolated quantity a variance: positive, bounded, and smooth between texels.
        // It loses the mixture's between-texel term, t(1-t)(meanA - meanB)^2 — which is precisely
        // the unstable part — so the stored variance slightly understates a texel boundary. An
        // understated variance is a STRICTER visibility test, which is the safe direction here.
        float dMean = dWeight > 0.0 ? dSum / dWeight : 0.0;
        float dVar  = dWeight > 0.0 ? max(d2Sum / dWeight - dMean * dMean, 0.0) : 0.0;
        sDepthTexel[lane] = dWeight > 0.0 ? vec2(dMean, dVar) : vec2(0.0);
    }
    barrier();

    // Every texel of the tile, interior and border alike, from shared memory — so the border never
    // reads an image the same dispatch is still writing.
    if (!ownsTexel) return;
    ivec2 src = blix_octBorderSource(texel, BLIX_OCT_TILE);
    vec3 value = sTexel[(src.y - 1) * BLIX_OCT_INTERIOR + (src.x - 1)];
    // <b>Converge, do not replace.</b> A solve used to overwrite the tile outright, which is
    // invisible for a probe that solves every `period` frames in a scene that has not changed — and
    // a hard step for one that has been asleep, because its stored answer is however old the sleep
    // was. Blending toward the new solution turns that step into a ramp of a few solves. The scene
    // is static and the sun moves by hand, so the lag this introduces for an awake probe costs
    // nothing; the smoothness it buys a waking one is the whole point.
    // <b>Converge from an answer, never from nothing.</b> Blending toward the new solution turns a
    // waking probe's jump into a ramp — but a probe that has NEVER solved holds the cleared tile,
    // and ramping from that is a fade up out of black, which is a worse artifact than the pop it
    // was meant to fix. The stored alpha is the marker: solves write 1.0, the clear leaves 0, so
    // the first solve of a probe's life takes its answer outright.
    vec4 previous = texelFetch(uAtlasPrev, tile + texel, 0);
    // <b>Alpha carries two things, and the encoding is what lets it.</b> It was the has-ever-solved
    // marker, needed so a first solve takes its answer outright instead of ramping up out of the
    // cleared tile. Mapping the sunlit fraction into [0.5, 1] keeps every solved texel above 0.25
    // and leaves the cleared value of 0 the only thing below it — so one channel answers "has this
    // probe solved" and "how much sun did it see" without either question blurring the other.
    float converge = previous.a > 0.25 ? SOLVE_CONVERGE : 1.0;
    imageStore(uAtlas, tile + texel,
               vec4(mix(previous.rgb, value, converge), 0.5 + 0.5 * sunlitFraction));
    vec2 moments = sDepthTexel[(src.y - 1) * BLIX_OCT_INTERIOR + (src.x - 1)];
    // <b>.b carries reachability, and it costs nothing.</b> The depth atlas is Rgba16F and has only
    // ever used .rg, so the flag rides along in memory already allocated and already fetched by
    // every consumer — no second texture, no second binding, no extra bandwidth. It is constant
    // across the tile, so the bilinear fetch that reads the moments returns it exactly.
    // Alpha carries the mean ray closure. It is constant across the tile, like the reachability
    // flag beside it, and the atlas is Rgba16F with .a never having been read — so the instrument
    // costs no texture, no binding and no bandwidth that was not already moving.
    imageStore(uDepthAtlas, tile + texel, vec4(moments, reachable, sMeanClosure));
    if (lane == 0) imageStore(uProbeUsage, probe, vec4(max(usage - decay, 0.0)));
}
